**W2-D7-Virtual_Environment**

---

**What and Why:**
Every Django project needs its own isolated environment. Without venv, all projects share same packages — if Project 1 needs Django 4.0 and Project 2 needs Django 5.0, they conflict and break each other. Venv creates a completely separate Python environment per project — each has its own packages, own versions, no conflicts. This is non-negotiable before starting Django next week.

---

```bash
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 1: CREATE AND ACTIVATE VENV
# ============================================================

# STEP 1 — Create virtual environment
# Open terminal, navigate to your project folder first
# cd Desktop/myproject  (or wherever you want)

python -m venv venv
# python -m venv = run venv module
# last 'venv' = name of the folder created
# creates folder called 'venv' with isolated Python inside
# you can name it anything but 'venv' is standard convention


# STEP 2 — Activate venv

# Windows:
venv\Scripts\activate

# Mac/Linux:
source venv/bin/activate

# After activation — you'll see (venv) at start of terminal line:
# (venv) C:\Users\Vijay\Desktop\myproject>
# (venv) = confirmation venv is active
# everything you install now goes INSIDE this venv only


# STEP 3 — Confirm venv is active
where python      # Windows — shows python path inside venv folder
which python      # Mac/Linux — same

# path should show something like:
# C:\Users\Vijay\Desktop\myproject\venv\Scripts\python.exe
# NOT the global Python path
```

---

```bash
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 2: INSTALL PACKAGES INSIDE VENV
# ============================================================

# Make sure (venv) shows in terminal before installing anything

pip install django
# installs Django ONLY inside this venv
# other projects not affected

pip install djangorestframework
# install DRF — needed for Week 4

pip install psycopg2-binary
# PostgreSQL connector — needed for Week 3

# Confirm Django installed:
django-admin --version
# shows Django version — e.g. 5.0.1
# if shows version = installed correctly
```

---

```bash
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 3: requirements.txt — SAVE AND RESTORE PACKAGES
# ============================================================

# Save all installed packages to file:
pip freeze
# shows list of all installed packages with exact versions:
# Django==5.0.1
# djangorestframework==3.14.0
# psycopg2-binary==2.9.9

pip freeze > requirements.txt
# > requirements.txt = saves output to file
# creates requirements.txt in current folder
# this file goes in GitHub — standard in every real project

# Restore packages from requirements.txt (on new machine or new venv):
pip install -r requirements.txt
# -r = read from file
# installs exact same versions as original project
# this is how teammates set up your project from GitHub
```

---

```bash
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 4: DEACTIVATE AND PROOF IT WORKS
# ============================================================

# Deactivate venv:
deactivate
# (venv) disappears from terminal
# now using global Python — not your project's venv

# Proof venv isolation works:
django-admin --version
# ERROR or different version — Django not installed globally
# proves Django was only inside venv, not system-wide

# Reactivate when needed:
venv\Scripts\activate    # Windows
source venv/bin/activate # Mac/Linux
# (venv) appears again — back inside isolated environment
```

---

```bash
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 5: FOLDER STRUCTURE AFTER SETUP
# ============================================================

# myproject/
# ├── venv/                ← virtual environment folder
# │   ├── Scripts/         ← activation scripts (Windows)
# │   ├── Lib/             ← installed packages live here
# │   └── ...
# ├── requirements.txt     ← list of installed packages
# └── .gitignore           ← tells Git to ignore venv folder

# IMPORTANT: NEVER push venv folder to GitHub
# venv folder = huge (hundreds of MB)
# requirements.txt = tiny text file with package list
# anyone cloning your repo runs: pip install -r requirements.txt
# they get exact same environment without downloading venv

# .gitignore file — create this file and add:
# venv/
# __pycache__/
# *.pyc
# .env
# This tells Git to ignore these files/folders
```

